answerParticipation.conceptSenatDesc:
At the National Assembly, each MP votes from their own desk, with their own key. They may hand their vote to a colleague only in the few cases the law allows, such as illness or a government mission, and that colleague may carry only one vote on top of their own. In practice, proxies are common, and the Assembly publishes which votes were cast this way. But an MP absent without a proxy is counted absent, and that is what produces the {an}%.

The Senate works differently. At an ordinary public ballot, votes are cast with named ballot papers, and a single senator per group can take them all to the urn. A resolution proposal tabled by a senator in 2022 describes this itself: one senator present drops in "the 100 or even 150 named tokens of their colleagues", even when no other member of their group is in the chamber. One person per group is enough for the whole group to vote. Public ballots held at the rostrum, where each senator is called by name, do not work this way, but the Senate's open data does not allow them to be singled out. Nor do solemn public ballots, decided by the Conference of Presidents: each senator votes for themselves, and for one colleague only if they hold that colleague's proxy. Those can be spotted, because the Senate publishes their named proxies, and theirs alone.

That is why the Senate records a position for almost all of its members at every ballot, and why it publishes no attendance figure at all. Its rate does not say who was there. It says whose ballot was cast.{contraste, select, oui {

Our data shows it in one line. A senator who belongs to a group on the day of the ballot votes {tauxAvecGroupe}% of the time. With no group, when they must cast their own ballot in person, {tauxSansGroupe}%. That is not a difference in diligence, it is the presence or absence of a group delegate.} other {}}

The two rates therefore measure different things. The least misleading comparison is the Assembly's solemn ballots ({solennel}%), announced in advance so that everyone can plan for them, against the Senate's votes ({sn}%).
